WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.240
I'm now going to do one of my favorite reactions.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:03.860
 

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:05.200
It's not very spectacular,

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:07.220
but I think it is very satisfying.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:12.180
It's the reaction between hydrogen peroxide and potassium permanganate.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.740
Let's begin with a bit of water.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.860
So I'll add some potassium permanganate,

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.880
which gives it a nice pinkish-purple colour.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:23.900
You've probably heard me saying already that,

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:28.480
my grandfather used to put permanganate in his goldfish tank,

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.660
so the fish would swim around in purple water.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.640
Didn't seem to do them any harm.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:37.200
We now add a little bit of sulfuric acid.

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:41.480
The acid doesn't change the colour at all.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:45.520
So you can see now we got some nice purple potassium permanganate,

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:50.580
and now we'll take some hydrogen peroxide.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:54.320
Sam had a big bottle that she needs to be used up.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.640
And we'll add it and see if we can see anything happening.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.440
So watch carefully.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.360
I think it may be flat.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.600
Try another bottle.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.040
We'll get some of my own hydrogen peroxide instead.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:17.780
Brady: Gone flat?

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:18.660
Yes.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:23.240
Well, hydrogen peroxide can decompose into oxygen and water.

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:25.860
And if you keep it too long, it goes off.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:27.200
So I'll get some more.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:34.520
So we got some more. There isn't really much of it but perhaps this will be enough.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:44.320
I'm not very impressed. Do you?

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.500
Brady: It's not the best thing I've seen.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:53.500
Hydrogen peroxide has the formula H2O2

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:56.460
And it has quite a strange structure.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:59.540
This is a model that I use for the students.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:03.120
I'm sorry that the atoms are all the same colour, but you imagine:

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:08.660
This one, and that one, the ones I'm holding with my hands, are hydrogens.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:12.460
And the two black ones are oxygen.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:18.300
And the important thing is you can see it has quite short hydrogen-oxygen bonds,

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:26.180
And a very long oxygen-oxygen bond, which is weak, and breaks very easily,

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:29.620
which is what makes hydrogen peroxide so reactive.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:33.680
It also has this rather strange structure,

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:38.580
that this bond is at right angles to that one.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:43.960
If I hold it up, this one is horizontal, that one is vertical.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:49.680
And so it has a rather strange symmetrical structure.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:56.180
I once hit myself very hard on the nose while demonstrating this in a lecture.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:02.680
So you have a long oxygen-oxygen bond that makes hydrogen peroxide very reactive.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:07.960
Pure hydrogen peroxide will set fire to almost all organic matter

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:13.360
So if you put it on a piece of cloth... Whoosh! It goes up in flames.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:18.440
So normally people use hydrogen peroxide as a dilute solution.

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:26.920
And here, you can see we have one solution which is called 40 volume,

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:35.280
which means 1 litre of liquid gives 40 litres of oxygen gas, if it decomposes

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:43.860
And this sample here, which you can see, has more aggresive warnings, more dangerous,

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:53.760
This is 100 volume, and so the hydrogen peroxide that you buy from the chemists for bleaching your hair is much weaker.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:59.300
It may only be 5 or 10 volumes, so this is 10 times stronger.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:03.140
You wouldn't bleach your hair with this, might go up in smoke.

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:06.320
Trying again. This time I hope a bit better

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:13.620
I put warm water in here so the reaction may be faster, and I'll put in most of the acid as well.

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:16.020
Then a bit of permanganate.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:21.860
(Brady laughs) And... Now we've got enough peroxide still left

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:24.740
You can see it's decolourising straight away.

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:26.580
(Chuckles)

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:28.080
That's pretty cool.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:29.800
(Brady laughs)

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:31.260
Brady: What's going on there?

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:36.000
So what's happened now, I didn't wash the thing out properly, and we had some peroxide left.

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:41.460
And now it's reacting faster than we can add the permanganate

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:42.300
(Chuckles)

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:45.040
So this is actually much better than I intended,

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:48.400
because I was going to make it purple and make it go clear,

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:53.500
and here you can see I'm pouring in a purple solution, and it's disappearing!

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:55.500
Brady: That's brilliant!

00:04:57.740 --> 00:05:03.080
And you can see, it's releasing oxygen from the hydrogen peroxide

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:11.660
So when it hits the surface, the manganese, which is bound to oxygen in permanganate,

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:18.900
reacts and loses its oxygen and forms manganese ions, manganese 2+ (Mn2+) , for those of you chemists,

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:21.660
which are completely colourless.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:25.580
So it goes from this very intensely coloured salt to the colourless one.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:27.580
So it's like magic, isn't it?

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:30.580
Brady: You'll have to remember this trick. Professor: Yes.

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:34.640
Brady: Lovely.

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:37.440
Brady: This wasn't what you wanted to show was it?

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:39.120
No, but it's even better

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:44.640
Then I now have to explain to the technicians why I've used the whole bottle of permanganate solution.

